{"ts": 1789782772.361229, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Evaluations are deterministic (no noise in repeated calls). Let me reason about what to submit.\n\n**Structure of the evidence.** Feature 28 is the dominant driver \u2014 it alone gives 1.112 out of the final 1.302. The three leading triples all share 28 and differ only in which two of {1, 12neg, 27} accompany it, scoring 1.286/1.295/1.302. That spread of 0.016 is far smaller than the uncertainty in any single Sharpe estimate, so the \"winner\" among them is essentially arbitrary \u2014 I should not believe 28+1+27 is truly better than the others.\n\n**Why I expect meaningful but not full persistence.** Two observations argue the signal is real rather than fitted:\n1. The sign structure is strongly asymmetric \u2014 flipping 28's sign collapses the Sharpe from 1.302 to 0.017, and flipping 1 or 27 costs ~0.6-0.7. A spec assembled from noise would not show such sharp directional structure.\n2. The pair and triple Sharpes are close to what independent diversification of the standalone signals predicts. Combining 28 (1.112) with two ~0.55 features lifts it only to ~1.30, which is the modest gain you'd expect from adding genuine-but-weaker independent signals \u2014 not the explosive jump that signals overfitting to idiosyncratic residuals.\n\n**Why I still discount substantially.** I ran ~70 evaluations and selected the maximum. With n=5000 daily observations, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/5000)\u00d7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 \u2014 sizeable. Selecting the best of many correlated candidates inflates the estimate by perhaps 0.1\u20130.2. Beyond pure selection noise, in-sample Sharpes degrade out-of-sample for structural reasons too: feature-return relationships drift, and the equal-weight combination was implicitly chosen to suit this particular sample.\n\nSo I'd center my belief around 0.85\u20130.95, a haircut of roughly 0.4 from the 1.302 in-sample figure, with wide uncertainty.\n\n**Choice of spec.** Rather than take the nominal max, I'll pick **28 + 1 + 27**, but note it's effectively tied with the alternatives. All three share feature 28 plus the two next-most-robust standalone performers (1 at 0.606, 27 at 0.539). I slightly prefer 27 over 12-negated because 27's positive sign requires no inversion, and positively-signed raw signals tend to be marginally more stable \u2014 a weak tiebreaker, but the candidates are otherwise indistinguishable."}
